{% import "design/_example.html.jinja" as ex %}
{% macro demos() %}
    <p class="ds-lead">
        A plain CSS component on native radios: one answer from a few, where each option needs a line saying what it means. The markup comes from <code>forms/choice_list.html.jinja</code>, included from Jinja or rendered from Templetor with <code>render_jinja_template</code>. An honest exit ("Not sure") is a row like any other, not a button somewhere else.
    </p>
    {% call ex.example("Default", "Nothing is picked in advance: the reader answers rather than confirms a default.", output_style="max-width: 28rem;") %}
        {% with name="demo-literary-form",
            labelled_by="demo-choice-label",
            rows=[
                {"value": "fiction", "label": "Fiction", "description": "Novels, stories, plays and poems."},
                {"value": "nonfiction", "label": "Nonfiction", "description": "History, biography, memoir, essays, how-to and reference."},
                {"value": "", "label": "Not sure", "description": "Hard to call, like a book based on a true story or a collection that mixes fiction and nonfiction."},
        ] %}
            <span id="demo-choice-label" hidden>Literary form</span>
            {% include "forms/choice_list.html.jinja" %}
        {% endwith %}
    {% endcall %}
    <div class="ds-notes">
        <p>
            <b>Source.</b> <code>static/css/components/choice-list.css</code>; import it on any page that renders the partial.
        </p>
        <p>
            <b>Click again.</b> Pass <code>again_hint</code> and the picked row shows it, for a form that treats a second click on the picked row as the save. The partial only shows the hint; the form's script does the submitting (the Tasks page's <code>initQuickNext</code> is the one example).
        </p>
    </div>
{% endmacro %}
